<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agamemnon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agamemnon is the legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> who, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, led the Greek army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. Son of Atreus and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>, he sacrificed his daughter Iphigenia for a fair wind and on his return was murdered by his wife Clytemnestra, a story dramatised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-schliemann">Heinrich Schliemann</a> named a gold funeral mask from Mycenae after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agamemnon.jpg" alt="Agamemnon" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jastrow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lekanis_Agamemnon_MNA_Taranto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek mythology Agamemnon was a descendant of Pelops, son of Tantalus. According to the common story, told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>, he and his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> were the sons of Atreus, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a>, and Aerope, daughter of the Cretan king Catreus. His father Atreus murdered the sons of his twin brother Thyestes and fed them to him. Thyestes' son Aegisthus then killed Atreus and took the throne of Mycenae. Agamemnon and Menelaus took refuge with Tyndareus, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, who brought them back to Mycenae, where they drove out Thyestes.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, Agamemnon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> married Tyndareus' daughters Clytemnestra and Helen. Menelaus succeeded Tyndareus as king of Sparta, while Agamemnon, with his brother's help, drove out Aegisthus and Thyestes to recover his father's kingdom, and he extended his dominion by conquest to become the most powerful prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>' Oresteia he has a son, Orestes, and two daughters, Iphigenia and Electra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Agamemnon gathered the Greek forces to sail for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. At Aulis, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, the army incurred the anger of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>, and plague and a lack of wind stopped the fleet. The prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchas">Calchas</a> announced that the goddess could be appeased only by the sacrifice of Agamemnon's daughter Iphigenia, and the classical dramatists differ on how willing father and daughter were.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> tells of his quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> in the final year of the war. Agamemnon took Chryseis as a war prize and, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> sent a plague, reluctantly returned her, then seized Achilles' prize Briseis in compensation. Achilles withdrew from the fighting. Agamemnon later sent ambassadors to Achilles with offers of riches and his daughter's hand, which Achilles refused until his companion Patroclus was killed by Hector. They were reconciled in Book Nineteen. He is described as a representative of kingly authority, whose chief fault was haughtiness, and as one of the three best warriors on the Greek side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, Cassandra, the daughter of Priam, fell to Agamemnon as a prize. On his return he was killed. In the oldest versions he was slain by Aegisthus, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Odyssey Aegisthus ambushes him at a feast. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> and the tragedians he is killed in a bath by Clytemnestra, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>' Oresteia. Orestes later avenged him by killing Aegisthus and his own mother, and was pursued by the Erinyes until he was absolved. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> he was worshipped as Zeus Agamemnon, and his tomb was pointed out among the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyclae">Amyclae</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agamemnon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mycenaean civilisation takes its modern name from Mycenae, the citadel in the north-eastern Peloponnese that Greek legend made the seat of King <b>Agamemnon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After nine years of fighting, <b>Agamemnon</b>, king of Mycenae and commander of the Achaeans, takes the captive Briseis from Achilles, the Achaeans' greatest warrior, who withdraws from the fighting and asks his mother Thetis to have Zeus let the Trojans drive the Achaeans back to their ships.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Ottoman and British representatives signed an armistice aboard HMS <b>Agamemnon</b> off Lemnos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew in the early Iron Age on a terrace above a harbour; myth said <b>Agamemnon</b> gathered the Greek chiefs there before Troy.</blockquote><a href="/state/aegium">Aegium</a></li><li><blockquote>Homer made it the seat of <b>Agamemnon</b>, and Schliemann's excavations from 1876 made it famous.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mycenae">Kingdom of Mycenae</a></li></ul></section>
